---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Stress Adaptation | Shabel Lab">
    <!-- Back Button -->
    <div class="navigation-aid">
        <a href="/research" class="back-link">&larr; Back to All Research</a>
    </div>

    <!-- Deep Dive Content -->
    <article class="research-detail">
        <h1>Behavioral Adaptation and Stress</h1>
        
        <div class="featured-image-placeholder">
            <p>Microscopy or behavioral data chart goes here</p>
        </div>

        <h2>Project Overview</h2>
        <p>A fundamental question in neuroscience is how the brain adapts to repeated negative outcomes. The lateral habenula (LHb) is highly sensitive to aversive stimuli and stress. Overactivity in this region is heavily implicated in the pathophysiology of depression and other mood disorders.</p>
        
        <p>Our lab investigates the exact synaptic and cellular changes that occur within the LHb following acute and chronic stress. By understanding these maladaptive changes, we hope to uncover new targets for therapeutic intervention.</p>

        <h2>Methodology</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Patch-Clamp Electrophysiology:</strong> Measuring synaptic strength and plasticity (such as AMPA/NMDA receptor ratios) in LHb slices from stressed vs. control models.</li>
            <li><strong>Behavioral Assays:</strong> Utilizing established rodent models of stress and depressive-like behaviors to correlate cellular changes with outward behavioral adaptations.</li>
            <li><strong>Molecular Interventions:</strong> Using pharmacology and targeted genetic tools to attempt to reverse stress-induced synaptic changes in vivo.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Current Objectives</h2>
        <p>We are currently investigating whether rapid-acting antidepressants can directly reverse the specific stress-induced synaptic potentiation we observe in the lateral habenula, effectively restoring normal circuit function and behavior.</p>
    </article>
</Layout>

